
## Demo vault

`demo-vault/` is this plugin's documentation: a real Obsidian vault whose notes explain each feature and
demonstrate it with `code-button`s that run TypeScript through
[CodeScript Toolkit](https://github.com/mnaoumov/obsidian-codescript-toolkit). `npm run version` archives it
into the GitHub release, injecting the freshly built plugin and the `demo-vault-helper` bootstrap plugin
into the archived copy — so nothing plugin-related is committed under `demo-vault/.obsidian/plugins/`.

When you add a user-facing feature, add or update its note in the same change.

### One-time setup before the button suite can run

`npm run test:integration:demo-vault` opens a copy of `demo-vault/` in a real Obsidian and clicks every
button in it. It needs the CodeScript Toolkit binary, which it copies out of your own `demo-vault/` to keep
the run hermetic. Open `demo-vault/` in Obsidian once and let `demo-vault-helper` install CodeScript
Toolkit; the installed copy is gitignored, so each machine does this for itself. Until then the suite fails
with a message telling you exactly this.

### Checking the vault

- `npm run test:integration:no-app` — the coverage suite. Reads the notes without launching Obsidian and
  enforces the authoring rules: an `# H1` and a prose opener per note, Markdown links rather than
  wikilinks, no `[Docs]` line, every note reachable from `00 Start.md`.
- `npm run test:integration:demo-vault` — the button suite. A button fails at click time, so this is the
  only gate that can catch a broken one.
